# {{projectName}}

A TinyGres starter application built with TypeScript and Vite.

This project was generated with the **{{adapter}}** adapter and **{{storage}}**
storage.

## Start it

{{#if isSupabase}}

1. Open your Supabase project's SQL editor and run `supabase.sql`. The script
   creates and seeds the disposable `public.tinygres_posts` demo table, grants
   anonymous read access, enables row-level security, and adds the table to
   Realtime.
2. Check `client/.env.local`. The wizard put the browser-safe project URL and
   publishable key there. Never replace the publishable key with a secret or
   service-role key.
3. Install and start the client:

```sh
cd client
npm install
npm run dev
```

If you edit `.env.local`, restart Vite so it loads the new values. Commit
`.env.example`, but do not commit `.env.local`.

## What the app demonstrates

TinyGres restores its {{#if usesOpfs}}OPFS cache{{else}}in-memory replica{{/if}}
inside a dedicated Worker, renders any locally available rows as soon as
`ready()` resolves, and then snapshots `public.tinygres_posts` through the
Supabase Data API. Supabase Realtime events invalidate the local table and the
page re-runs its parameterized query. `whenSynced()` confirms that the complete
remote baseline is live before the final reconciled render.

The built-in Supabase source is deliberately read-only. It uses a browser-safe
publishable key and assumes every row in this demo table is visible to the
anonymous role through the table-wide `USING (true)` policy installed by
`supabase.sql`. It does not send local writes upstream and does not support
secret keys, authenticated per-user visibility, or complete PostgreSQL SQL
compatibility.

{{#if usesOpfs}}
The logical OPFS cache name is `{{storageName}}`. TinyGres binds it to the
Supabase URL, publishable key, and selected table contract so a changed source
opens a different physical cache. If you change the database policy or
publication without changing those values, also change the logical cache name
or clear the old site data. OPFS requires a secure browser context and is
reconstructable cache storage, not a backup.
{{else}}
Memory storage starts empty on every page load. The Supabase snapshot fills it
again before synchronization becomes live.
{{/if}}

## Demo database cleanup

The SQL file intentionally creates a narrowly named public demo table. Remove
it when you are finished experimenting:

```sql
do $cleanup$
declare
  demo_table regclass := to_regclass('public.tinygres_posts');
begin
  if demo_table is not null then
    if obj_description(demo_table, 'pg_class') is distinct from
      'create-tinygres:v1 disposable public demo table'
    then
      raise exception using
        message = 'Refusing to drop unowned public.tinygres_posts';
    end if;
    drop table public.tinygres_posts;
  end if;

  if exists (
    select 1
    from pg_publication
    where pubname = 'supabase_realtime'
      and obj_description(oid, 'pg_publication') =
        'create-tinygres:v1 disposable demo publication'
  ) and not exists (
    select 1
    from pg_publication_tables
    where pubname = 'supabase_realtime'
  ) then
    drop publication supabase_realtime;
  end if;
end
$cleanup$;
```

The guarded publication cleanup runs only when this starter created the
publication and it contains no remaining tables.

{{else}}

```sh
cd client
npm install
npm run dev
```

The app creates TinyGres in its default dedicated module worker, loads a small
{{#if usesOpfs}}persistent OPFS{{else}}in-memory{{/if}} snapshot, and runs a
parameterized PostgreSQL-shaped query locally.
The button simulates a normalized incoming server change so you can see table
invalidation and re-querying end to end.

This starter is deliberately read-only and network-free. `replaceTable` and
`applyBatch` are source-integration APIs, not application write APIs. It does
not configure Supabase, upstream writes, or complete PostgreSQL SQL
compatibility.

{{#if usesOpfs}}
The OPFS cache is named `{{storageName}}`. Reloading restores it before the app
queries, and the demo seed is installed only when the database is new. OPFS
requires a secure browser context and is reconstructable cache storage, not a
backup.
{{else}}
Memory storage starts with the same sample snapshot on every page load.
{{/if}}
{{/if}}
